        <section id="so-who-will-be-looking-at-these-written-communications-and-reports?"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>So who will be looking at these written communications and reports?</h2>
              <p>In an evolving threat landscape it is important that everyone within an organisation is kept informed by written communication as to various aspects of cyber security and any threats within their company or organisation. However, certain employees will need access to more detailed written communications and reports than others.
Let's look at the different level of employees and what kind of written communication will be aimed at them.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Line manager
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Line managers hold meetings to communicate information to their teams but they also communicate regularly via email. They will regularly read any reports and use the information from these to action via written communication to all team members such as with an impending threat or potential vulnerability.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u6_s3_p2_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Senior management
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Senior management use a variety of different types of communication including more formal written communication in the form of messages, orders, instructions, reports, memos etc. Written communication can make sure there is paper trail to follow especially when something is important such as a potential threat or investigation after an incident.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/placeholder_icon.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Team members
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Team members may communicate with each other in different ways especially informally, but the best way to make sure that everyone gets the same information is via written communication. Distributing reports to the team enables them to keep up to date with all issues, and written communication offers opportunity for better clarity and transparency especially when it comes to actioning against a possible threat.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u6_s3_p2_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          All staff
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>All staff can benefit from written communication from bulletins giving organisational and strategic updates or memos on the current cyber security alert. Different types of reports can be used to inform different departments concerning specific information or more general information that affects the whole organisation. Written communication can be targeted specifically to certain areas or to all areas but can give the same information to everyone depending on how much they need to know.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u6_s3_p2_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
